LESSON 12: STYLING THE GROCERYLIST ITEM
================================================================================
PART 1: OVERVIEW OF THE LESSON
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL LEARN IN THIS LESSON
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson focuses on creating and styling a reusable GroceryItem component.

You will understand:

• Creating a reusable GroceryItem component

• Designing an item-card layout

• Passing item data through props

• Passing event-handler callbacks through props

• Styling purchased and unpurchased states

• Creating category badges

• Displaying quantity and prices

• Styling Edit and Delete actions

• Adding hover and focus feedback

• Creating responsive item cards

• Improving keyboard accessibility

• Respecting reduced-motion preferences

• Keeping presentation separate from list-management logic


WHY THIS LESSON IS IMPORTANT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each grocery item is one of the most frequently viewed and interacted-with
parts of the Grocery List App.

A well-designed item component should make it easy to understand:

• What the item is

• How many units are required

• Which category it belongs to

• What one unit costs

• What the total line cost is

• Whether it has been purchased

• How to edit it

• How to delete it


The overall component flow is:

GroceryList
    ↓
item + callbacks
    ↓
GroceryItem
    ↓
Styled Card
    ↓
User Interaction
    ↓
Callback to Parent
    ↓
State Update
    ↓
Updated GroceryItem


================================================================================
PART 2: UNDERSTANDING ITEM STYLING
================================================================================

WHAT IS ITEM STYLING?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Item styling controls the visual presentation of one grocery record.

It includes:

• Layout

• Spacing

• Typography

• Colors

• Borders

• Status indicators

• Buttons

• Focus states

• Hover states

• Responsive behavior

• Motion/transition behavior


================================================================================
GOALS OF GOOD ITEM STYLING
================================================================================

A good GroceryItem should provide:

1. CLEAR HIERARCHY

   Item name should be easy to identify.


2. STATUS VISIBILITY

   Purchased items should visibly differ from active items.


3. READABLE METADATA

   Category, quantity, and price should be easy to scan.


4. CLEAR ACTIONS

   Edit and Delete should be distinguishable.


5. RESPONSIVE BEHAVIOR

   The card should remain usable when horizontal space becomes limited.


6. KEYBOARD FEEDBACK

   Interactive controls should have visible focus indicators.


7. MOTION CONTROL

   Animations should respect users who prefer reduced motion.


